The Security Council,

Recalling its resolutions 2653 (2022), 2699 (2023), 2751 (2024), 2752 (2024), 2785 (2025) and 2793 (2025), and the relevant statements of its President,

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity and unity of Haiti, and stressing that the Government of Haiti bears primary responsibility for providing security, ensuring stability and respect for the rule of law and human rights, and protecting civilians, with the support of the international community,

Expressing grave concern at the continued deterioration of the security and humanitarian situation in Haiti, including gang violence, mass displacement, acute food insecurity, attacks on schools, hospitals, humanitarian personnel and medical facilities, and the closure of humanitarian operations by Doctors Without Borders in Port-au-Prince,

Condemning in the strongest terms all violations and abuses of human rights and international humanitarian law, including attacks against civilians, children, women and girls, sexual and gender-based violence, recruitment and use of children, killing and maiming, abduction and denial of humanitarian access, and underlining the need for accountability,

Stressing the need for unhindered, safe and timely humanitarian access to all populations in need, and emphasizing that humanitarian actors must be protected and allowed to operate in accordance with humanitarian principles,

Welcoming the transition of the Multinational Security Support mission to the Gang Suppression Force pursuant to resolution 2793 (2025), and commending Kenya, troop- and police-contributing countries, the Caribbean Community, the Organization of American States and all partners for their efforts in support of Haiti,

Reaffirming that sanctions are a targeted instrument in support of peace and stability, and stressing that the measures renewed by this resolution are not intended to harm the civilian population or to impede humanitarian assistance,

Expressing serious concern at the illicit trafficking and diversion of arms, ammunition and related materiel, which continue to fuel gang violence and undermine the sovereignty and stability of Haiti, and calling for strengthened regional and international cooperation to address those flows,

Determining that the situation in Haiti continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security in the region,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Reaffirms its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity and unity of Haiti, and calls upon all actors to respect the authority of the Government of Haiti and to refrain from any action that undermines stability;

2. Condemns in the strongest terms the continuing gang violence and attacks against civilians and humanitarian personnel in Haiti, and demands the immediate cessation of such attacks and unhindered, safe and timely humanitarian access to all populations in need;

3. Calls upon the Government of Haiti, with the support of the Gang Suppression Force (GSF), the Haitian National Police, the Haitian armed forces, the United Nations Integrated Office in Haiti (BINUH) and the United Nations country team, to take urgent measures to protect civilians, in particular children, women and girls, and to ensure accountability for violations and abuses;

4. Welcomes the authorization of the GSF in resolution 2793 (2025) and calls for its full and swift operationalization, in close coordination with the Government of Haiti, the Haitian National Police, the Haitian armed forces, BINUH and the United Nations country team, and urges Member States and partners to provide voluntary contributions, personnel, logistics and other support to the GSF;

5. Stresses that the GSF shall carry out its mandate in strict compliance with international law, including international human rights law and international humanitarian law, as applicable, and with the United Nations Human Rights Due Diligence Policy, and shall take fully into account the protection of children and other vulnerable groups;

6. Decides that the measures imposed by paragraphs 1, 2 and 3 of resolution 2653 (2022) shall be renewed until 17 October 2026;

7. Decides that the measures referred to in paragraph 6 above shall also apply to individuals and entities designated by the Committee established pursuant to resolution 2653 (2022) (the “Committee”) for planning, directing, or committing acts involving the obstruction of humanitarian assistance, attacks against civilians, United Nations personnel, humanitarian workers, medical personnel, or schools and hospitals, sexual and gender-based violence, the recruitment and use of children, or the illicit trafficking or diversion of arms and related materiel in or into Haiti;

8. Reaffirms the arms embargo imposed by resolution 2653 (2022) and calls upon all Member States to take appropriate measures to prevent the direct or indirect supply, sale or transfer of arms, ammunition and related materiel, including technical assistance, training, financial or other assistance, to individuals and entities designated by the Committee and to armed groups or criminal networks designated by the Committee;

9. Urges Member States, in particular those in the region, to strengthen border, maritime and air controls, to cooperate in preventing the illicit trafficking and diversion of arms and related materiel to Haiti, and to share relevant information with the Committee and the Panel of Experts;

10. Reaffirms that the measures renewed by this resolution shall not prevent the provision of humanitarian assistance or the delivery of basic services, and requests the Committee to consider promptly and in accordance with its guidelines any exemption requests, including those related to humanitarian activities;

11. Decides that the measures renewed by this resolution shall not apply to arms, ammunition and related materiel intended solely for the support of or use by United Nations personnel, the GSF, the Haitian National Police, the Haitian armed forces, or other security providers authorized by the Government of Haiti and notified to the Committee, as appropriate;

12. Requests the Committee to consider additional designations, to update its sanctions list and to report to the Security Council on its work, and encourages the Committee to cooperate closely with the GSF, BINUH, the Organization of American States and the Caribbean Community;

13. Decides to extend the mandate of the Panel of Experts established pursuant to resolution 2653 (2022) until 17 November 2026, and requests the Panel to submit to the Security Council a midterm report no later than 15 April 2026 and a final report no later than 15 September 2026, and to provide informal updates as appropriate;

14. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Security Council, no later than 15 January 2026, on the implementation of resolution 2793 (2025), the humanitarian situation and access in Haiti, and the cooperation among the GSF, BINUH and the United Nations country team;

15. Calls upon all Member States to comply fully with the measures renewed by this resolution, and urges regional organizations and international partners to continue to support Haiti’s own efforts to restore security, protect civilians, strengthen the rule of law and prepare for free and fair elections;

16. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

