Determining that the situation in Haiti continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security in the region,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Reaffirms its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity and unity of Haiti, and calls upon all actors to respect the authority of the Government of Haiti and to refrain from any action that undermines stability;

2. Condemns in the strongest terms the continuing gang violence, including terrorist activities, and attacks against civilians, humanitarian personnel and medical facilities in Haiti, and demands the immediate cessation of such attacks and unhindered, safe and timely humanitarian access to all populations in need;

3. Calls upon the Government of Haiti, with the support of the Gang Suppression Force (GSF), the Haitian National Police, the Haitian armed forces, the United Nations Integrated Office in Haiti (BINUH) and the United Nations country team, to take urgent measures to protect civilians, in particular children, women and girls, and to ensure accountability for violations and abuses;

4. Calls for an inclusive, Haitian-led and Haitian-owned political process, with the good offices of BINUH and in coordination with regional partners, aimed at preparing free and fair elections and restoring democratic institutions, and requests the Secretary-General to report on progress in this regard;

5. Takes note of the authorization of the GSF in resolution 2793 (2025) and calls for its operationalization in strict accordance with that resolution and in full respect for the sovereignty, territorial integrity, political independence and unity of Haiti, with the consent and close coordination of the Government of Haiti, the Haitian National Police, the Haitian armed forces, BINUH and the United Nations country team, and without expanding its mandate; stresses that this coordination should take place under the overall coordination of the Special Representative of the Secretary-General; urges Member States and partners to provide adequate, predictable and sustainable voluntary contributions, personnel, logistics and other support to the GSF, and encourages the Standing Group of Partners to coordinate such support; and requests the Secretary-General to report on financing and logistical gaps;

6. Stresses that the GSF shall carry out its mandate in strict compliance with international law, including international human rights law and international humanitarian law, as applicable, and with the United Nations Human Rights Due Diligence Policy, and shall take fully into account the protection of children and other vulnerable groups, including through the implementation of the 2024 Handover Protocol between the United Nations and the Government of Haiti; and shall establish appropriate compliance, oversight and accountability arrangements, including robust risk assessment, monitoring and reporting mechanisms, and shall ensure that children associated with armed gangs are treated as victims and referred to child protection actors and reintegration programmes, that children apprehended in operations are referred to child protection actors and not detained with adults, and that survivors of sexual and gender-based violence are referred to protection and support services;

7. Decides that the measures imposed by paragraphs 1, 2 and 3 of resolution 2653 (2022) shall be renewed until 17 October 2026;

8. Decides that the measures referred to in paragraph 7 above shall also apply to individuals and entities designated by the Committee established pursuant to resolution 2653 (2022) (the “Committee”) for planning, directing, or committing acts involving the obstruction of humanitarian assistance, attacks against civilians, including killing and maiming and abduction, attacks against United Nations personnel, humanitarian workers, medical personnel, or schools and hospitals, sexual and gender-based violence, the recruitment and use of children, the illicit trafficking or diversion of arms and related materiel in or into Haiti, illicit trafficking in narcotics, or money laundering, or activities that destabilize Haiti through the illicit exploitation or trade of natural resources; and decides that designations under this paragraph shall be based on clear, credible and substantiated evidence, and that the Committee shall provide a summary of reasons for listing and ensure fair and clear procedures for delisting, in accordance with its guidelines;

9. Reaffirms the arms embargo imposed by resolution 2653 (2022) and calls upon all Member States to take appropriate measures to prevent the direct or indirect supply, sale or transfer of arms, ammunition and related materiel, including small arms and light weapons, including technical assistance, training, financial or other assistance, to individuals and entities designated by the Committee and to armed groups or criminal networks designated by the Committee;

10. Urges Member States, in particular those in the region, to strengthen border, maritime and air controls, to cooperate in preventing the illicit trafficking and diversion of arms and related materiel to Haiti, and to share relevant information with the Committee and the Panel of Experts;

11. Reaffirms that the measures renewed by this resolution shall not prevent the provision of humanitarian assistance or the delivery of basic services, and directs the Committee to consider expeditiously and in accordance with its guidelines any exemption requests, including those related to humanitarian activities, to process such requests without undue delay through clear, expedited, streamlined and transparent procedures, including for humanitarian actors and medical personnel, and requests the Committee to report to the Council on any unintended humanitarian impact of the measures;

12. Decides that the measures renewed by this resolution shall not apply to arms, ammunition and related materiel intended solely for the support of or use by United Nations personnel, the GSF, the Haitian National Police, the Haitian armed forces, or other security providers authorized by the Government of Haiti, subject to prior notification to the Committee and appropriate safeguards to prevent diversion, including end-use documentation, monitoring and tracing;

13. Requests the Committee to consider additional designations expeditiously, to update its sanctions list and to report to the Security Council on its work, and encourages the Committee to cooperate closely with the GSF, BINUH, the Organization of American States and the Caribbean Community;

14. Decides to extend the mandate of the Panel of Experts established pursuant to resolution 2653 (2022) until 17 November 2026, and requests the Panel to submit to the Security Council a midterm report no later than 15 April 2026 and a final report no later than 15 September 2026, and to provide informal updates as appropriate; and requests the Panel to include in its reports information on arms trafficking and diversion networks, including arms seizures notified by the GSF and the Haitian National Police;

15. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Security Council, no later than 15 January 2026, on the implementation of resolution 2793 (2025), including on the operationalization of the GSF and progress on implementation of the sanctions measures; compliance by the GSF with international law, including international human rights law and international humanitarian law, and the United Nations Human Rights Due Diligence Policy; child protection measures and protection of civilians; the human rights and child-protection impact of the GSF’s operations; the humanitarian situation and access in Haiti; cooperation among the GSF, BINUH and the United Nations country team; the implementation of the measures renewed by this resolution and any humanitarian impact; measures taken to prevent the diversion of arms and related materiel; funding, deployment and operational readiness and results of the GSF; and progress towards a Haitian-led political process and free and fair elections;

16. Calls upon all Member States to comply fully with the measures renewed by this resolution, and urges regional organizations and international partners to continue to support Haiti’s own efforts to restore security, protect civilians, strengthen the rule of law and prepare for free and fair elections;

17. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

